  I was spending over $200 every single month on SAS subscriptions and honestly it was killing my budget. Between productivity tools, design software, grammar checkers, and scheduling platforms, the costs just kept piling up. Then I discovered something that genuinely changed everything. There are completely free alternatives out there that work just as well and sometimes, believe it or not, even better. Over the past year, I replaced five expensive SAS tools with free options and saved hundreds of dollars annually. Today, I am sharing exactly which tools I replaced, what free alternatives I use instead, and how you can slash your SAS spending all the way down to zero. Number one, Notion Plus, $10 per user per month, replaced by Obsidian, completely free. All right, let's start with Notion. The Plus plan costs $10 per user per month built annually. And look, once you need unlimited file uploads, extended page history, or team collaboration, that's where the costs really start creeping up. So, I replaced it with Obsidian, which is completely free for personal use and honestly, in my opinion, better for my needs. Now, here's what makes Obsidian special. It's a local first note-taking app that stores everything on your device as plain markdown files. You own your data completely. No cloud dependency, no subscription fees ever. And what makes Obsidian truly exceptional is the linking system. You just create connections between notes by typing double brackets. And over time, this builds an entire web of interconnected ideas. The graph view even visualizes how your whole knowledge base connects together. It's genuinely impressive. For project management, I use free community plugins. The conbon plugin gives me a Trello style board. The calendar plugin organizes my daily notes. The tasks plugin builds out a powerful to-do system. These replicate everything I ever used. used notion for, but faster and with zero monthly cost. You can sync for free using Dropbox or Google Drive. I've been using Obsidian for 14 months now with over $800 notes and I've spent exactly $0. Zero. Number two, Grammarly Pro. $12 per month replaced by Language Tool completely free. Okay, next up is Grammarly Pro. Now, this one costs $12 per month on the annual plan, which works out to $144 per year. And look, Grammarly is useful, no question. But that price adds up fast. So, I switched to Language Tool, and it offers a genuinely generous free plan that handles everything I need. Language Tool is an open-source grammar and style checker that works across 30 plus languages. The free version checks up to 10,000 characters per text field, which is more than enough for emails, blog posts, and most documents. It catches grammar mistakes, punctuation errors, style issues, and suggests improvements just like Grammarly does. I installed the browser extension, and it just works everywhere. Gmail, Google Docs, WordPress, anywhere I write online. What I appreciate most is how well it handles formal writing and multiple languages at the same time. The premium version does exist at around $5 per month, but honestly, I've never once felt the need to upgrade. Language Tool handles my entire writing workflow without costing me a single penny. Number three, Canva Pro, $120 per year, replaced by Canva Free Plus Photoia. Both completely free. Now, let's talk design because this one surprised me. Canva Pro costs $120 per year on the annual plan or $15 per month if you pay monthly. And while I genuinely loved the premium templates and extra features, I realized, wait, I can replicate 95% of what I need using Canvas free plan combined with something called Photoia. And here's the thing, Canvas free tier is genuinely impressive on its own. You get access to over 1.6 6 million free templates, drag and drop design tools, and the ability to create social media graphics, presentations, and posters without spending a dime. Yes, there are some limitations like fewer premium templates, no background remover, and a smaller stock library. But for that remaining 5%, Photoia steps in. And this tool is honestly a hidden gem. It's a completely free online photo editor that works like Photoshop, right in your browser. No installation required. It supports PSD files, layers, masks, and all the advanced editing you would expect from professional software. The workflow is dead simple. Do your layouts and social graphics in Canva free. Then hop over to Photoia when you need advanced editing or more precise control. Together, these two free tools replace absolutely everything I used Canva Pro for. That's $120 saved every single year without touching design quality. Number four, Calendarly Standard. $10 per user build annually, replaced by Cal.com, completely free. All right, let's move on to scheduling. And this one is a no-brainer swap. Calendarly's standard plan costs $12 per user per month or $10 per month build annually. That's $120 a year just for unlimited event types, branding removal, and some basic workflow integrations. I replaced it with Cal.com, and this one is open-source and gives you all of that completely free. Cal.com is honestly better in several ways, not just cheaper. Even on the free plan, you get unlimited event types, calendar integrations with Google Calendar and Outlook, custom availability settings, and automated email reminders. But here's what really sets it apart. On Cal.com, you can remove their branding, use a custom domain, and access workflow automation, all on the free tier. On Calendarly, those same features cost you extra. I connected it to my Google calendar, set up different meeting types for consultations, quick calls, and client sessions, and it just works flawlessly. The booking page looks completely professional, and clients have had zero issues scheduling with me. Setup took about 15 minutes and I've been running on it for 9 months without a single payment. Number five, Loom Business. $15 per month replaced by OBS Studio completely free. And finally, screen recording. Loom's business plan runs about $15 per user per month, which adds up to roughly $180 per year for unlimited recording, no watermarks, longer recording times, and editing features. I replaced it with OBS Studio and honestly there's no comparison. OBS is free and it's far more powerful. OBS Studio is professional-grade software used by content creators, streamers, and professionals worldwide when they need serious recording capabilities. Now, yes, the learning curve is steeper than Loom. I won't pretend otherwise, but once you get it set up, it's on a completely different level. With OBS, I can record my screen, webcam, or both simultaneously with fully custom layouts. I can switch between scenes, add overlays, pull in audio from multiple sources, and record in high quality with no watermarks, and absolutely no time limits. For quick recordings, I just fire up a simple scene and hit record. The output quality is better than Loom, and I can record for as long as I need without any restrictions whatsoever. Eight months in, dozens of tutorial videos and client presentations, and I haven't paid a single cent. The total savings, $684 every single year. So, let's add this all up because the numbers are pretty eye opening. Switching from Notion Plus to Obsidian saves $120 per year. Moving from Grammarly Pro to Language Tool saves $144 per year. Replacing Canva Pro with Canva free plus Photoia saves $120 per year. Dropping Calendarly Standard with Cal.com saves $120 per year. And swapping Loom Business with OBS Studio saves $180 per year. Add it all up and that's $684 in annual savings. over 5 years. We're talking over $3,400 that stays in your pocket instead of going to subscriptions. And here's the part I really want you to hear. My productivity didn't drop, not even a little. In some cases, like with Obsidian and OBS, I actually gained capabilities I never even had before. These free alternatives are actively maintained. They have strong communities behind them, and they're not going anywhere. So, there you have it. Five expensive SAS tools completely replaced with free alternatives, saving nearly $700 every year. Replace Notion Plus with Obsidian for note-taking. Swap Grammarly Pro with Language Tool for grammar checking. Use Canva Free Plus Photoia for design. Switch from Calendarly Standard to Cal.com for scheduling and replace Loom Business with OBS Studio for screen recording. Each switch takes a little time to get set up and get comfortable with, but the long-term savings make it absolutely worth it. Stop paying for features you can already get for free and start putting that money towards actually growing your business instead. Let us know in the comments which SAS tool you're planning to replace first. And don't forget to like, share, and subscribe for more money-saving tech tips.
  
  
  
